+# Decision: Change delivery over self-hosted WebSockets
+
+## Context
+
+MarketBoard editors submit commands over HTTPS; committed changes must appear in connected browsers within 15 seconds under normal connectivity. Three delivery designs can meet that floor: five-second HTTPS polling, self-hosted WebSockets, or wrapping a hosted realtime service. All three can also satisfy the recovery goal (a browser disconnected for up to 20 minutes regains the latest board) and the scale goals (800 simultaneous browsers, 20 edits per second at opening bursts) — so none is eliminated on those grounds. The choice is which trade the project prefers to live with.
+
+What each option changes is what the project owns. The board is already a self-hosted HTTPS server: commands, authorization, and the event board run on infrastructure the coordinators operate themselves. A hosted realtime provider would add a standing bill and a third party to the delivery path of a coordinator-run weekend service; owned connection management is the alternative cost. This decision determines what the change store, delivery, and browser-sync nodes are sized around.
+
+## Decision Elements
+
+Every option meets the 15-second freshness floor, the 20-minute recovery goal, and the scale goals, so those are background invariants rather than decision elements. The elements that drive selection:
+
+- **Latency below the freshness floor** (moderate): subsecond updates are useful but not required. Faster than 15 seconds is headroom, not a new requirement.
+- **Operational burden** (major): connection capacity and reconnect handling must be owned and run — by coordinators staffing weekend events, not a dedicated ops team.
+- **Store read load at scale** (major): 800 browsers polling every five seconds is roughly 160 sustained store queries per second regardless of edit rate; push-based designs read only on catch-up.
+- **Recurring service fees** (major): a hosted service bills monthly for connections and messages. A standing vendor bill for delivery is a cost and dependency the project is deliberately not taking on.
+- **Provider coupling** (major): a hosted service's API, SDK, pricing changes, and outages enter the delivery path; moving away later means replacing delivery machinery in both server and browsers.
+- **Browser-side complexity** (moderate): what staff browsers must run — a polling loop with cursor catch-up, or socket reconnect and catch-up logic.
+
+## Options in the Domain
+
+### Five-second HTTPS polling
+
+Browsers request changes since a stored cursor every five seconds; the store serves cursor queries; delivery stays stateless request/response.
+
+- Latency below the floor: **partial** — committed changes appear with up to five seconds of lag plus request time.
+- Operational burden: **strong** — no connection state; a missed poll is simply the next poll.
+- Store read load: **weak** — roughly 160 queries per second at 800 browsers, sustained whether or not anything changed.
+- Recurring fees: **strong** — none.
+- Provider coupling: **strong** — none.
+- Browser complexity: **partial** — a polling loop is simple, but the 20-minute recovery goal still requires cursor-based catch-up, and a stalled connection must be displayed as such or a silent gap reads as an unchanged board.
+
+Summary: simplest to operate; pays in repeated store reads and a few seconds of lag.
+
+### Self-hosted WebSockets
+
+The server holds a socket session per connected browser and pushes each committed revision as it is committed. The change store keeps revisions replayable so a reconnecting browser can catch up; the delivery component owns connection capacity and the reconnection of dropped sessions; browsers run reconnect and catch-up logic against the revision history.
+
+- Latency below the floor: **strong** — push delivery lands committed revisions in connected browsers near-instantly.
+- Operational burden: **weak** — connection capacity, session state, and reconnect handling are all owned.
+- Store read load: **strong** — reads happen on catch-up, not on a timer.
+- Recurring fees: **strong** — none.
+- Provider coupling: **strong** — none.
+- Browser complexity: **partial** — reconnect and catch-up logic is real work, though it leans on the same replayable revisions that any option needs for the recovery goal.
+
+Summary: fast updates with no vendor; pays in owned connection operations.
+
+### Hosted realtime service
+
+The store publishes committed revisions to a managed realtime provider; the provider fans out to browsers and handles their reconnection; delivery wraps the provider and reconciles publication (the store confirms what the provider has); browsers subscribe through the provider's model.
+
+- Latency below the floor: **strong** — push delivery, provider-managed.
+- Operational burden: **strong** — the provider owns connection capacity and reconnects.
+- Store read load: **strong** — same as sockets.
+- Recurring fees: **weak** — a monthly bill sized by connections and messages.
+- Provider coupling: **weak** — the provider's API, SDK, pricing, and outages become part of the board's delivery path.
+- Browser complexity: **partial** — the subscription model is provided, but it is the provider's model, not the board's.
+
+Summary: quickest low-latency route; pays in fees and coupling.
+
+## Selection
+
+Self-hosted WebSockets.
+
+Latency below the floor is only a moderate criterion, so polling was not rejected for being slow — it was rejected for what its simplicity costs elsewhere: sustained repeated store reads at full browser scale, and lag that is structural rather than occasional. The hosted service was rejected on the two major criteria it fails: recurring fees and provider coupling. The board already runs on self-owned infrastructure for commands over HTTPS, so the marginal burden of sockets lands on infrastructure the project operates anyway. The trade is real and accepted deliberately: connection capacity and reconnect handling are owned, by coordinator-run operations, as the cost of fast updates without tying the board to a realtime vendor.
+
+The tenet permitting both self-hosted and managed infrastructure is not contradicted — the choice leans to the self-hosted side of that permission. The connectivity-visibility tenet (failures must be visible, not mistaken for an unchanged board) binds whichever route runs.
+
+Committing forecloses stateless delivery: the delivery and browser-sync nodes are socket-shaped from here. It obligates downstream: the change store must provide replayable revisions and reliable publication; delivery must own session management, connection capacity, and reconnection of dropped sessions; browser sync must own reconnect and catch-up logic. The freshness goal stays at 15 seconds — the subsecond capability this buys is headroom, not a new requirement.
